22:05:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really smart and fair point, and it goes right to the historical heart of why this stereotype feels so baked-in. You’re absolutely right: the neurologist Georges Gilles de la Tourette, who gave the syndrome its name, did describe a patient who swore uncontrollably. And that’s not a minor detail — that’s the origin story. So doesn’t that mean coprolalia is fundamentally what Tourette syndrome *is*? Let’s look at that history carefully, because it actually reveals the opposite of what it seems to on the surface. ### The Marquise de Dampierre: the famous case In 1885, Gilles de la Tourette published a study of nine patients. The most famous was a French noblewoman, the Marquise de Dampierre, who indeed had coprolalia — she would involuntarily shout obscenities, which was shocking in polite society. This case was dramatic, highly memorable, and became the centerpiece of his paper. But here’s the crucial part: **she wasn't the only patient in that original paper.** The other eight patients had a variety of motor and vocal tics, and not all of them had coprolalia. In fact, even in that foundational 1885 description, coprolalia was present in only a subset of the cases. Gilles de la Tourette himself defined the condition by the combination of motor tics and at least one vocal tic, along with a waxing-and-waning course — not by swearing. ### Why did coprolalia become the “core” in the public mind? Because the Marquise’s symptom was so socially explosive, it overshadowed everything else. The medical community and later the public latched onto the swearing as *the* spectacular, defining feature. Over time, the condition became reduced, in the cultural imagination, to that one symptom. But that’s a distortion of the original medical picture, not a faithful reflection of it. ### Baked into the origin story, but not into the condition’s core Think of it this way: the Wright brothers' first plane is remembered for that moment of flight at Kitt